  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 110 AM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nFriday - Saturday: For the end of the week and into the start of the \nweekend, some active weather may persist for south central and \nsoutheastern Colorado. Another wave is expected to push across the \nregion through this timeframe, but there is some uncertainty in how \nthe wave will evolve, primarily in will it be a more open wave or \nmore compact wave like the Wednesday-Thursday one. As of now, \ncurrent leaning is more open, which ensemble guidance supports, \nleading to higher confidence in that pattern progression. With this \npattern, a brief uptick in ascent can be expected, which may help \nsupport isolated to widely scattered shower development across the \nregion. The greatest coverage of showers can be anticipated along \nthe mountains, where any forcing will be maximized. With that all \nsaid, any showers present would be expected to dissipate through \nSaturday, as the wave drops to the south/east and forcing lessens. \nOtherwise, partly to mostly cloudy skies can be expected, with winds \nremaining relatively light around 10-15 mph. As for temperatures, \nthey will also remain fairly stable, with much of the area hovering \naround to slightly above seasonal values.\n\nSunday - Tuesday: For the end of the weekend and into early next \nweek. Quiet weather makes a return to south central and southeastern \nColorado. Overall, ridging is expected to make a return, bringing \nincreased subsidence and drier air to the area. Given this, dry \nconditions are anticipated through this period. With that said, \nthere are signals of another wave passage through Tuesday, but \nconfidence in that feature impacting the area is very low (10-20%) \nat this time. Otherwise, mostly clear to partly cloudy skies and \nlight winds can be anticipated. Temperatures through this period \nwill also start a warming trend, with much of south central and \nsoutheastern Colorado warming to above seasonal values.",
